%[text] ## Exercise: Integral of a decaying exponential
%[text] Consider the integral of an exponentially decaying optical signal.   
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(t)=\\int\_{0}^{T} I(0)\\,e^{-t/\\tau}\\,dt = I(0)\\,\\tau\\left(1 - e^{-T/\\tau}\\right)$
%[text] Compare the exact version of this integral.  Use an initial Intensity of 1, and a decay time of 10 ns.    Integrate from 0 to 5 ns.   
%[text] The optimized quadratic approximation (Gauss quadrature) uses a value of  $f = \\sqrt\\frac{3}{20}$  (the fraction of the interval) in the following expression 
%[text] ![](text:image:520d) 
%[text] ## Exercise 1Work Area
%[text] Add your code for Exercise 1 here.  The result should look something like this:
%[text] 
% Define the parameters
T = 5;
tau = 10;
I0 = 1;

% Define the function and its exact integral
II = @(t) I0.*exp(-t/tau);
Exact_Integral = @(T) I0.*tau.*(1-exp(-T/tau));
% Exact value
int_exact = Exact_Integral(T) %[output:891fd3f6]

% Choose the number of intervals and the interval width
Npts = 15;
Delta = T/Npts;

% Define the sample points
ts = Delta/2:Delta:T-Delta/2 %[output:57174da8]

% compute the midpoint (trapezoidal approximation)
int_trap = Delta.*sum(II(ts)) %[output:57ea8c69]

% Now compute the quadratic approximation for the integral 
ff = sqrt(3/20);
int_quad = int_trap + (Delta./(24.*ff.^2)).*sum(II(ts+ff.*Delta)+II(ts-ff.*Delta)-2.*II(ts)) %[output:15adc065]

% Find the errors for each method
trap_err = int_trap - int_exact %[output:1405697c]
quad_err = int_quad - int_exact %[output:91e74a8b]


